            ("What point on the stress-strain curve represents the maximum engineering tensile stress that a material can withstand before localized necking begins?", "Ultimate Tensile Strength (UTS)", ["Yield Strength", "Fracture Strength", "Proportional Limit"], "UTS is the peak point on the engineering stress-strain curve; beyond UTS, cross-sectional area thins rapidly into a localized neck."),
            ("What dimensionless ratio measures the transverse contraction strain to longitudinal extension strain when a material is stretched in tension?", "Poisson\'s Ratio (nu)", ["Young\'s Modulus", "Shear Modulus", "Bulk Modulus"], "Most engineering metals have a Poisson\'s ratio around 0.3; stretching a cylinder causes its diameter to contract proportionally."),
