            ("What safety boundary surrounding energized electrical parts requires unqualified personnel to remain outside unless escorted by an electrical worker?", "The Limited Approach Boundary", ["The Restricted Approach Boundary", "The Arc Flash Boundary", "The Prohibited Approach Boundary"], "NFPA 70E defines boundaries to prevent shock; crossing the limited approach boundary requires qualified training and PPE."),
            ("What closer shock boundary requires qualified workers to wear insulated voltage-rated rubber gloves and protective equipment before crossing?", "The Restricted Approach Boundary", ["The Limited Approach Boundary", "The Outer Perimeter", "The Maintenance Buffer"], "Entering the restricted boundary puts body parts near exposed energized circuits, requiring certified insulated rubber gloves with leather protectors."),
            ("How often must electrical insulating rubber gloves be electrically re-tested in a certified laboratory under OSHA 1910.137 regulations?", "Every 6 months (or every 12 months if unopened in factory sealed boxes)", ["Every 2 years", "Every 5 years", "Once during lifetime of glove"], "Dielectric testing every 6 months ensures that microscopic pinholes, chemical degradation, or ozone cracking have not compromised glove insulation."),
            ("What field safety inspection must an electrician perform on voltage-rated rubber gloves every day prior to putting them on?", "Visual inspection and an air-inflation roll test to detect microscopic punctures and leaks", ["Soaking gloves in warm soapy water", "Wiping gloves with chemical solvents", "Measuring glove thickness with a caliper"], "Rolling the cuff traps air inside the glove, expanding the fingers; holding the glove to the ear reveals hissing air leaks through pinholes."),
            ("Why must leather protector gloves always be worn directly over voltage-rated rubber insulating gloves during electrical work?", "To protect the delicate rubber insulating layer from mechanical punctures, tears, cuts, and abrasions", ["To provide thermal warmth to the electrician's hands", "To provide additional electrical insulation", "To make the gloves fit more snugly"], "Thin rubber gloves are easily punctured by wire ends and sharp metal edges; leather outer gloves provide rugged mechanical shielding."),
