            ("What should you do if you are in a vehicle and a downed high-voltage electrical power line falls directly across your car?", "Stay inside the vehicle and warn others to stay away, calling 911 until utility crews de-energize the line", ["Step out of the car immediately one foot at a time", "Grab the wire and fling it off the vehicle", "Crawl out on your hands and knees"], "The vehicle chassis and rubber tires form an equipotential Faraday cage; stepping out with one foot on the car and one on the ground is fatal."),
            ("If your vehicle catches on fire after a power line falls on it and you MUST evacuate, what is the only safe evacuation procedure?", "Jump cleanly out of the vehicle without touching car and ground simultaneously, landing with both feet together and bunny-hopping away", ["Step down carefully onto the running board", "Slide down the hood onto the ground", "Run away in long, leaping strides"], "Jumping cleanly prevents completing a circuit between vehicle and ground; bunny-hopping with feet together eliminates lethal step potential."),
            ("What physical safety rule should an electrician follow when operating an industrial manual disconnect switch or knife switch?", "Stand to the side (off to the hinge side), turn your face away, close your eyes, and operate the handle with a swift, decisive motion", ["Stand directly in front of the box and look inside", "Operate the handle as slowly as possible", "Hold the box door open while operating"], "Standing to the side keeps the electrician's body out of the explosive blast path in case an internal arc fault blows the enclosure door open."),
            ("What hazard is created when an electrical neutral conductor is disconnected or 'lost' in a 120/240-Volt split-phase residential electrical service?", "Unbalanced line-to-neutral voltages fluctuate wildly (e.g., one phase surges to 180V while the other drops to 60V), destroying electronics", ["All power shuts down completely in the home", "The main circuit breaker trips immediately", "Water pipes become cold"], "A shared neutral carries unbalanced return current; losing it creates a 240V series circuit where lighter loads experience destructive overvoltage."),
            ("Why should water never be used to extinguish a Class C energized electrical fire?", "Water contains dissolved minerals that conduct electricity, creating an immediate electrocution path back to the firefighter", ["Water causes electrical wires to corrode instantly", "Water causes circuit breakers to seize up", "Water generates poisonous fluorine gas"], "Liquid water conducts high-voltage current directly into the nozzle and firefighter; non-conductive CO2 or dry chemical extinguishers are required."),
            ("What class of fire extinguisher is specifically rated for fires involving energized electrical equipment?", "Class C fire extinguishers", ["Class A extinguishers", "Class B extinguishers", "Class D extinguishers"], "Class C ratings certify that the extinguishing agent (carbon dioxide, dry chemical powder) is electrically non-conductive."),
            ("What is the primary danger of using an ordinary non-certified ladder when performing electrical work around overhead lines?", "Aluminum and metal ladders conduct electricity, creating a direct path to ground if they contact energized overhead lines", ["Metal ladders are too heavy to balance safely", "Metal ladders trigger circuit breakers from a distance", "Metal ladders attract lightning strikes indoors"], "Only fiberglass or non-conductive wooden ladders with clean, dry side rails are authorized for electrical work."),
